WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.610
- Welcome to Rostovzteff lecture,

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:11.180
sponsored by the Rostovzteff Fund

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.223
in the Classics Department at Yale.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.580
It's lovely to have such a large

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.280
and such an international audience.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.403
It's the silver lining of Zoom.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:24.440
So I'm gonna speak very briefly just to

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.940
sort of introduce the lecture.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.620
And some of you have
heard this story before,

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.220
but when I was first chair happily,

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.550
it hasn't been all along.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:37.210
But the first time in 2007,

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.340
our wonderful administrative
assistant came to me one day

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.260
and she said, "do what
this Rost, Rost, Rost

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:44.000
this new fund is?"

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.290
And I said, "I do not."

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.400
So we looked and we learned
how to pronounce the name

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.400
and discovered that Rostovzteff's widow

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.300
had left us a substantial amount of money

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:05.077
in a proper endowment and with nothing,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:07.900
no strings on it,

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.350
except that it was to support
the teaching and research

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.473
of ancient history and archeology at Yale.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.330
And ancient history at Yale in 2007

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.360
was not nearly as strong as it is now.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.570
And it seemed to me that a
good thing to do would be

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.570
to advertise and to give split
some wind under its wings.

00:01:27.956 --> 00:01:32.956
So I started this lectureship
and then later a Rostovzteff

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.170
traveling fellowship in archeology,

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:37.810
which has supported graduate students

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:41.150
from a number of different
departments over the years.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.800
And of course, ancient history at Yale

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.410
is now flourishing and
Joe Manning, Noel Lenski,

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:51.470
Andrew Johnson and Jessica
Lamont all work together every

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:55.890
year to pick the lecturer
and to do activities around

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.113
the annual lecture.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.740
So you all know who Mikhail
Ivanovich Rostovtzeff was

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.390
and how important his work has been

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.300
in shaping the various ways

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.930
of doing ancient history and archeology.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.300
He came to Yale by a circuitous route,

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:11.960
the collapse of the Romanov empire

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.880
and the triumph of Leninist bolshevism,

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.370
drove him from Russia in
1918, accompanied by his wife.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.970
He spent two years in Oxford in 1920.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.880
He was offered a chair at
the University of Madison

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.450
University of Wisconsin, Madison,

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:26.850
where he remained for what he termed

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.160
the happiest years of his life.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.010
In 1925, he came to Yale where

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.000
he was made Sterling
Professor of Ancient History

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.400
and classical archeology.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.900
And in 1927, he undertook and directed

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.310
the Yale excavations at Dura-Europos

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:42.500
which of course had a profound
impact on the composition of

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.150
the classics department here
for the next few decades.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.173
And on archeology of the,

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.340
we don't call it late
antiquity at that point, do we,

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:58.340
but of the empire and going
on from the fall of Dura

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.379
for many many years.

00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:03.157
William Calder wrote of him in the,

00:03:04.327 --> 00:03:07.660
"The Biographical Dictionary
of North American Classicists"

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.940
said he showed Americans
how to get things done.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:11.370
He frightened most of them.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.130
He never became say the
lecturer president of the APA,

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:15.410
or even chairman at Yale,

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:16.890
but he was (speaks in foreign language)

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.500
in the sense that nothing
will be the same after him.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.723
And there are a few of those
of whom that may be said.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.850
Tonight's lecturer is one
of whom that might be said,

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:29.570
Jessica Lamont we'll introduce him,

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:31.870
but I want to record a personal
pleasure that this year's

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:33.280
lecturer is Roger Bagnall,

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.210
who was a terrific guidance
and support to me when we were

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:39.373
rebuilding the Yale NELC department.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.273
So with no further ado, I turn
it over to Jessica Lamont.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.960
- Thank you so much, Chris.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.390
Hi everyone my name is Jessica Lamont.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.730
I'm an assistant professor
in the Department

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.131
of Classics here at Yale.

00:03:56.131 --> 00:03:59.760
Am hellenist as Chris mentioned
in our combined program

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.153
in ancient history.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.620
And it's really a pleasure
tonight to welcome

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.220
and introduce Roger Bagnall,

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:12.220
who has graciously stuck with
us as his lecture transformed

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:16.130
from an in-person event in which
she was meant to present on

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.220
a grand stage and then attend
to reception and a dinner

00:04:21.435 --> 00:04:26.435
with real live humans into
this remote event on Zoom,

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:29.710
which at least comes with a big audience

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:31.590
from around the world.

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.550
So thank you, Roger for this,

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:37.133
and thanks to all of you in
attendance with us tonight.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:41.120
So Roger was an undergraduate student

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.680
in the Department of
Classics here at Yale,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.540
where he graduated with
various honors and distinctions

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:51.180
before going on to receive his MA and PhD

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.730
at the university of Toronto.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.860
He spent the bulk of his career 33 years

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:01.260
as J professor of Greek
and Latin and professor

00:05:02.519 --> 00:05:04.690
of history at Columbia University,

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:08.130
during which you served as
Dean of the Graduate School of

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:12.970
Arts and Sciences and chair
of the department of classics.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:16.930
He then joined the NYU faculty in 2007

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.190
as a professor of ancient history

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.210
and the director of the Institute

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.133
for the study of the ancient world.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:28.410
In many ways, Roger is the
ideal of Rostovtzeff lecturer

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.170
as he like Rostovtzeff is a titan

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:32.863
in the field of ancient history.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:37.960
And he specializes in the
social and economic history

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:41.493
of the Hellenistic Roman
and late antique worlds.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:47.180
Roger's work is also interdisciplinary
combining documentary

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:50.380
evidence, especially that from Papyri,

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.960
with archeological evidence
to aluminate complex social

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:57.740
histories and like Rostovtzeff said

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.420
Roger also undertakes
and directs field work.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:05.900
Roger leads NYU's excavation
project at Amheida jointly

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:10.223
sponsored with Columbia
in the Dakhla in Egypt.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:11.998
His recent book "An Oasis City"

00:06:11.998 --> 00:06:16.397
presents some results from this work .

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:21.077
Now among Roger's other
well-known books are

00:06:21.077 --> 00:06:23.297
"Egypt in Late Antiquity"

00:06:23.297 --> 00:06:26.677
"Reading Papyri, Writing Ancient History,"

00:06:26.677 --> 00:06:29.250
"The Oxford Handbook of Papyri"

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:32.647
and "Everyday Writing in
the Greco-Roman East."

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:37.360
And while his publications
are extensive and have been

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:41.240
extremely influential in
ancient history circles,

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:45.820
I thought I'd call attention
to a less well-known article or

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:50.820
Rogers written in the year 1986 called

00:06:50.897 --> 00:06:54.247
"The Computer and Classical Research."

00:06:55.140 --> 00:07:00.140
Now in this a 34 year old
article, which I read last night,

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:03.810
Roger takes readers through
the exciting potential

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:07.560
for research and learning
that the computer

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.863
could have for the future
of classical scholarship.

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:16.020
34 years ago, Roger
speculated on the benefits

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:19.990
that technology it might
one day have for our field,

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:23.360
from the existence of
machine readable files to

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.613
the dissemination of the
results of one's research.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:28.550
Now I couldn't help,

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:33.480
but wish that past Roger
could see Roger tonight

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.650
here in the current moment,

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:39.430
presenting from his computer
to an audience across

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:42.470
the world, sharing images and texts

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:45.403
from his ongoing research
with all of us tonight.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:50.570
So without further ado,
here's Roger Bagnall,

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:55.550
speak to us on the other caravan
cities, transport capital,

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:59.540
and inequality in the Egyptian oases.

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:00.373
Thank you.

00:08:01.776 --> 00:08:02.776
- Thank you.

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:06.370
It is true that without technology,

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:09.393
I would just be having
dinner at home tonight,

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.810
which wouldn't be so bad, but
anyway, thank you very much,

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:17.113
Jessica and thank you to the
department for inviting me.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:23.270
It is surely among the least
of the many dismal consequences

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.060
of the COVID-19 pandemic that I am unable

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:29.260
to be speaking today in
the Yale lecture hall

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:30.523
to a live audience.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:35.060
I'm in fact in St. Louis
trying like most grandparents

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:38.743
that seems to help with my
grandchildren's remote schooling.

00:08:39.670 --> 00:08:42.020
And yet I feel the loss of presence

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:45.210
when I was back for my 50th reunion,

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:48.240
two years ago, many memories came back

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:52.470
of my undergraduate formation
in the school of Rostovtzeff

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.403
Can I have the slide please, Joe.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:58.100
It was a period of bitter struggle

00:08:58.100 --> 00:08:59.630
in the classics department,

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:03.690
whether that legacy would
survive, but through it all,

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:09.680
I learned much about what it meant

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:14.250
to be an ancient historian
from Rostovtzeff academic hair

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:17.500
C. Bradford Wells and Wells's, students,

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.310
John Oates, and Alan Samuel,

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:24.270
both of whom had left
Yale before I graduated.

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:27.440
And also from Jerry Polete
not only from his teaching,

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:30.140
but from my two years of working for him

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:32.790
in the department slide collection,

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:35.423
which had a profound impact on my career,

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:39.520
it is a joy to see ancient
history archeology so strong

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:41.533
at Yale in this century,

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:50.224
Rostovtzeff's caravan cities
was published in 1932.

00:09:50.224 --> 00:09:53.340
It was the translation of
a substantial rewriting

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:57.120
and expansion of a Russian
version of his travel sketches

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.060
published the year before.

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:01.560
And it was based on further
travel in the near east.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.763
After the appearance
of the Russian version,

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:06.630
publishing must have moved faster

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.723
before modern technology intervened.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:12.810
The preface tells us
that the choice of cities

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:16.400
was accidental based on
the state of preservation

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:20.153
of the ruins, Petra Jerash,
Pell Myra, and Dora.

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:24.790
Actually it is not an accident
that Damascus Aleppo mountain

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:26.930
were less susceptible to study.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:30.023
Because they had modern cities
on top of the ancient sites,

00:10:31.383 --> 00:10:33.900
Rostovtzeff's choice of what is today,

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:37.200
Jordan and Syria for his
travels was also a reflection

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.080
of his interests.

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.590
And of course of Yale's involvement

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:45.413
in the excavations at both
Dora and Jerash at the time.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:50.390
The underlying premise of the
book and the source of the

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:54.160
concept of caravan cities
is that the ancient cities

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:56.360
of the near east were
never self-sufficient.

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:00.260
They always had to trade
supply their needs for goods,

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:01.593
not available locally.

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:05.560
Although some of this
trade could go by water.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:07.960
Much of the interior of the
region could not be reached

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:10.200
except over land.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:11.410
The cities studied in the book,

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:16.260
all met that criterion as indeed
would have those he omitted

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:20.630
Rostovtzeff thus began with
an emphasis on the needs

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:23.580
of producers and consumers and the role

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.363
of caravan trade in meeting those needs.

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:30.470
It is all the more striking to see

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:33.130
that in the remainder
of the historical survey

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:35.170
that opens the book and even more in the

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:39.820
succeeding chapters, the agency
and motives of producers,

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:43.300
consumers and merchants are largely elided

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:45.740
in favor of the role of government.

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:49.223
In effect, the economics of
the trade got little attention,

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.823
but a moment's thought most
show that only if the trade

00:11:52.823 --> 00:11:56.063
economically viable with
those governments who cared,

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:00.913
how did the economics work and
what were the consequences?

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:05.070
By the time we get to Jerash
where Rostovtzeff admitted

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:07.570
that there was no direct
evidence of role for trade

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:09.220
in the city's obvious wealth.

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:11.210
Next slide, please.

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:14.540
The absence of any other
visible source of prosperity

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:16.940
was sufficient in his eyes to justify

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:21.330
the characterization of it
as a caravan city, the books,

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:24.270
origins and travel sketches
become increasingly clear

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:26.040
as it goes on.

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:29.460
Economic analysis is unusually

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.883
for Rostovtzeff barely present.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.630
There is these near Eastern
travels did not include Egypt,

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:41.073
although he did visit
it on other occasions,

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:43.330
Egypt does not however,

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:45.623
entirely missing from caravan cities.

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.940
Rostovtzeff stresses,
the close connections

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:50.690
between Egypt and the near east from

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:55.440
at least the fourth millennium
BC on, but he saw correctly,

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:59.220
the transportation inside
Egypt was above all by water on

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:02.590
the Nile and so by the first
millennium, and indeed,

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:05.410
I would say earlier,
was its external trade,

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:08.260
which used particularly
the maritime routes

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:09.703
to the ports of the Levant.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:14.500
He also pointed to the role
of Overland caravans and

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:17.150
connecting his caravan cities to Egypt,

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:18.580
mainly through the neck of land,

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:20.853
connecting the Nile valley to Sinai,

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:25.350
but no Egyptian city
figures as a caravan city

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:28.940
in his account, this is
perhaps not surprising

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:30.150
because the Egyptian cities

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:32.550
known to him were all
on the Mediterranean,

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:34.673
the Red Sea or the Nile.

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:38.340
Those with which I shall be
dealing were hardly known at the

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:42.860
time he wrote it is perhaps
telling that the word Oases does

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:45.483
not even figure in the index to the book.

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:50.443
One has to remember the
travel conditions of the era.

00:13:50.443 --> 00:13:54.130
Rostovtzeff missed visiting
Petra two decades earlier

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:57.400
because quote, "it was
necessary to equip a caravan,

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:59.630
to find a guards, and
to obtain and give a day

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:02.470
from the Turkish officials," unquote,

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:05.960
he lauds the English who have
quote "like the Romans before

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:07.640
them brought order and safety,

00:14:07.640 --> 00:14:11.223
good roads and new means
of transport" and of quote.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:16.157
But Petra was relatively easy
to reach compared to the Oasis

00:14:16.157 --> 00:14:20.680
of Egypt's Western desert, apart
from a few early explorers,

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:24.400
there was no archeology in
these oases before Ahmed Bakari

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:29.130
began work in the 1960s
and the Oases occupied the

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:32.410
most modest of places in
the ancient written sources

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:34.773
that were known in Rostovtzeff's time.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:38.023
Next slide, please.

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.780
There are five principle Oases in

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:45.080
the Western desert of Egypt
warming an arc that begins with

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:47.690
Siwa in the north continues
through Bahariya and Farafra

00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:50.753
in the middle and finishes
with Dakhla and Kharga.

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:56.030
The distances that stake are
large, even in modern terms,

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.620
about 300 kilometers from Paraitonion

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:02.520
modern Marsa Matruh on the coast to Siwa.

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:05.230
About 400 kilometers from Siwa to Bahariya

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:08.203
175 from Bahariya to Farafra

00:15:08.203 --> 00:15:10.690
300 from Farafra to Dakhla

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:13.533
and just under 200 from Dakhla to Kharga.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:18.260
Similarly distances from any
of them to the valley are vast

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:21.900
ranging from a low of about
200 kilometers from Bahariya

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:26.280
to Oxyrhynchus to 365
kilometers by the direct route

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:29.030
from Dakhla to the valley and

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:31.860
much the same from
Bahariya to modern Cairo

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:33.870
or ancient Memphis.

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:37.360
Siwa is in a class by itself, even one,

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:39.350
one reaches the Siwa Paraitonion.

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:41.510
It was still another 300 kilometers

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:44.023
along the coast to Alexandria.

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:49.610
Geologists will tell you,

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:53.690
even if not asked that
Oasis is a misnomer.

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:57.100
These are depressions in the
high desert plateau where the

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:59.940
ground level is several hundred
meters closer to the water

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:02.120
table than on the plateau.

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:03.343
Next slide please.

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:06.010
In pre-history and early history,

00:16:06.010 --> 00:16:07.723
there were artesian Springs.

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:10.060
At the Roman period water

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:13.480
depended on extraction
via wells and cannots

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:16.970
those underground channels
running downhill from aquifers.

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:18.173
Next slide, please.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:23.260
Only the area around these
water sources are there outlets

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:25.610
can be cultivated and would meet

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:28.100
the modern concept of an Oasis.

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:32.423
There are many, many such
smaller oases in each depression,

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:36.150
but this theological purism
does not actually correspond to

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:39.143
the meaning of Oasis and
either Egyptian or Greek,

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:43.210
just as an English.

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:45.740
The ancients used it as we do to refer

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:47.743
to the whole of a depression.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:53.410
My focus will be on the Oasis
and their relationship to

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:56.100
Egypt that is the Nile valley.

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:56.933
In other words,

00:16:56.933 --> 00:17:00.100
on the Oasis as destinations
and points of origin for

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:03.683
caravan traffic as
producers and consumers,

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.820
the degree to which they
were also waste stations

00:17:07.820 --> 00:17:11.330
on longer caravan routes
and thus more analogous

00:17:11.330 --> 00:17:13.800
to the classic caravan cities

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:18.800
as Rostovtzeff's book
portrayed them has been

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:21.670
debated to the south.

00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:23.660
The focus has been on
the route later known

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:27.380
as the Dab Al Dabain the 40 days track,

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:30.570
which in the medieval and
Ottoman periods was a major route

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:32.600
for trade in gold and slaves

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:35.363
from Sub-Saharan Africa
to the Mediterranean.

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:40.343
It ran south from the tip of
the Kharga Oasis to Darfur,

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:44.760
but its use is not clearly attested

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:46.333
before the medieval period.

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.470
That does not mean it was not at all.

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:52.820
We should be open to the
kind of re-evaluation that

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:54.180
Andrew Wilson has brought

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:56.610
to trans Saharan trade through the Fazan

00:17:57.650 --> 00:18:02.650
But even that was not as Wilson
has shown a through trade

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:06.800
route driven by demand in
Africa or in the Roman world.

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:08.520
As in later times,

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:11.660
rather it involved the need
on the part of the Garamantian

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:15.700
agricultural civilization to
export it surplus and import

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:16.563
what it lacked.

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:19.810
The Garamantians did
not pass on Roman goods

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:21.783
to its trading partners to the south.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:26.910
The residents of the Egyptian
oases are the counterparts

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:28.193
of the Garamantians.

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:33.070
With connections to the west.

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:35.550
We are somewhat better informed.

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:37.850
Already in the fifth
century, a route between

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:41.050
the Fazan and Siwa via
our Algila is attested by

00:18:41.050 --> 00:18:44.603
Haraditus taking, we're
told about 20 days.

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:48.110
Next slide, please.

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:51.450
It is for reasons I shall come
to not likely this route was

00:18:51.450 --> 00:18:55.680
in use much before that
time from Siwa, of course,

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:56.797
one could then reach Baharia

00:18:56.797 --> 00:18:59.193
And from there the volume
in the Nile valley,

00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:04.140
one medieval source places the
Fazan at 60 days travel via

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:08.363
this route to Cairo, perhaps
a slight exaggeration.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:13.760
There is very little evidence
whether literary documentary

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:17.890
or archaeological for active
traffic along this route in the

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:18.903
Roman period.

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:23.090
The very scarce spines of
African red slip wares and the

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:26.240
great Oasis tell us
nothing about the route

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:28.020
by which they arrived,

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:30.870
which is at least as likely
to have been by coastal trade

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:34.450
from Tunisia, transport up
the Nile and Oasis traffic

00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:37.470
with the valley, as it is
directly across the desert

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:39.733
via Siwa and the chain of Oasis.

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:43.770
And there's nothing so far
identified in the fines or

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:47.900
culture of the Oases to suggest
strong connections across

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:51.743
the Sahara or with Libyan
nomadic populations.

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:03.880
The possibilities of economic
life in the Oasis were driven

00:20:03.880 --> 00:20:07.870
above all by two constraints
that were also opportunities,

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:09.993
water and distance.

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:14.730
We'll talk further about each of these,

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:18.080
but first it is worth
reflecting briefly on the other

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:22.203
resources that the
Oases has had or lacked,

00:20:24.070 --> 00:20:28.990
they had soil from the breakdown
of rock and from solids

00:20:28.990 --> 00:20:30.800
and irrigation water,

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:34.040
but it was at perennial risk
of destruction either by the

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:38.148
occasional flash flood,
something documented Aldare

00:20:38.148 --> 00:20:40.630
in the Kharga Oasis or more

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:43.660
commonly by being blown
away by the north wind,

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:45.080
a perennial fact of life,

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:48.083
but apparently more vigorous
in some periods than others.

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:53.050
We had probably lost more than
a meter of ancient settlement

00:20:53.050 --> 00:20:54.810
debris at M Hayden.

00:20:54.810 --> 00:20:56.000
The site in Dakhla

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:59.393
where I have directed an
excavation since 2004,

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:03.933
retaining agricultural soils
was thus a permanent challenge.

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:10.570
There was however, plenty of sand.

00:21:10.570 --> 00:21:12.730
This is perhaps not a surprise to anyone,

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:14.743
but it should not be taken for granted.

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:22.490
The same north wind that carries
away top soil brings down

00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:26.330
sand dunes from the plateau
into the Oasis depressions and

00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:29.310
moves them steadily
southward at a good clip

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:30.700
about seven meters a year.

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:32.640
As we have measured it at M heda.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:33.733
Next slide, please.

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.653
These dunes vary in size,
but some are very large.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:42.430
This will give you an idea of the scale.

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:44.153
The next slide please.

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:49.003
Plantations such as orchards.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:53.910
The agricultural middle-class
seems not to have existed

00:21:55.970 --> 00:22:00.970
sixth, instead we find
landlords from tenants,

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:03.970
but not as in the valley
tenants with some independence

00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:06.580
negotiating time, limited leases,

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:10.350
rather tenants apparently
existing in continuing dependent

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:14.030
relationships with the
owners of the Wells.

00:22:14.030 --> 00:22:15.440
And of course in this context,

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:19.410
landlord means someone who
controls water and extracts rent

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:20.243
from it.

00:22:21.820 --> 00:22:22.653
And seventh,

00:22:22.653 --> 00:22:24.880
the capitalists behind the
Wells were to some degree

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:25.933
absentees.

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:30.900
Some of them undoubtedly
lived in Hibis Memphis,

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:32.600
which is the capital of Bahariya

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:36.050
but others lived in the valley.

00:22:36.050 --> 00:22:39.680
They depended on a managerial
class at multiple levels,

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:42.233
much as the wealthiest
landlords in the valley.

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:46.070
The higher managers might well
even be members of the city

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:48.070
council themselves.

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:52.210
And some of them may have
owned shares and Wells too.

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:54.270
The terminology of our texts does not

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:55.620
make it easy to tease out

00:22:55.620 --> 00:22:59.270
these complex and
hierarchical relationships.

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:01.900
But we can at least say that
management played a larger role

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:05.360
in the economy than regions
where independent farmers and

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:09.160
small urban landowners were
major factors as they were in

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:09.993
the valley.

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:14.680
These managers were of necessity literate.

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:16.233
So education was needed.

00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:20.723
Now some second order consequences,

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:24.090
the written documentation from the Oases

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:26.903
before the fourth century
is relatively scanty.

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:30.170
And the fourth century and
beginning of the fifth also

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:33.023
formed the period best
known from archeology.

00:23:34.550 --> 00:23:35.530
For various reasons

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:38.370
I do not think this was the
most prosperous period of these

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:41.260
islands of habitation and
the picture I'm giving of the

00:23:41.260 --> 00:23:46.260
society of the Oasis is largely
of its last phase before

00:23:46.310 --> 00:23:50.560
a rapid decline with the
abandonment of many sites,

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:52.960
it is possible that things were
somewhat different than the

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:55.053
first three centuries of Roman rule,

00:23:55.960 --> 00:23:59.260
but because the underlying
structural basis of the society

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:02.290
was already present from the
start of the Roman period,

00:24:02.290 --> 00:24:04.750
if not before, I think that most of

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:06.740
what I am saying about it was true

00:24:06.740 --> 00:24:08.593
throughout the Imperial period.

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:13.500
I've argued that the society
lacked the great middle groups

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:16.400
that we know from
Ptolemaic enrollment Egypt.

00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:17.233
On the one hand,

00:24:17.233 --> 00:24:20.040
the traditional Royal farmers
are public farmers with

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:24.010
long-term tenancy of land
owned by the crown or the state

00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:27.960
enjoying many protections
on stability of conditions.

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:30.670
They had paid a relatively high
proportion of their crop to

00:24:30.670 --> 00:24:32.650
the state and it doesn't matter whether

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:35.840
we call that rent or
tax, but the state knew

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:37.100
that its revenues dependent

00:24:37.100 --> 00:24:39.600
in large part on this population.

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:41.000
And it usually made sure that

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:44.430
they were not dispossessed
or seized for debt.

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:46.513
Creditors could not take their land.

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:48.633
On the other hand,

00:24:48.633 --> 00:24:50.800
perhaps a third of the rural
population owned their own

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:53.583
farms of sufficient size
to support a family.

00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:57.703
These two groups are not
attested in the Oasis.

00:24:59.162 --> 00:25:01.783
What we have instead is
five important groups.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:06.550
First, the tenants working the
land, watered by the Wells.

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:10.400
We know them almost
entirely, (Roger coughs)

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:12.943
excuse me, from texts,

00:25:13.890 --> 00:25:17.250
mainly Ostrica and the Kelis account book,

00:25:17.250 --> 00:25:19.440
which mentioned them in
connection with the products of

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:22.570
these Wells, particularly on brief texts,

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:25.330
written as tags from jars of wine.

00:25:25.330 --> 00:25:26.483
The next slide, please.

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:31.270
Second, the transport workers

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:33.940
whose importance I've already stressed,

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:36.700
donkey drivers and camel
drivers responsible for

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:40.780
the raising care and
exploitation of these animals,

00:25:40.780 --> 00:25:45.780
as well as wagon ears, third
artisans and service personnel,

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.600
mostly of groups
well-known from the valley.

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:51.710
Potters, smith's builders,
furniture makers,

00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:54.340
cobblers, Lyndon weavers,
buller's, millers,

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:56.910
retail merchants, bath
attendance, locksmiths,

00:25:56.910 --> 00:25:59.180
butchers, tailors, undertakers,

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:02.170
and the like along with
the more distinctive Oasis

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:04.343
occupation of well cleaner.

00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:07.440
Fourth the managers,

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:10.660
both the local agents well-known
from the documents and the

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:14.890
higher level supervisors
and fifth, the capitalists,

00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:16.900
mostly landlords goo quiet.

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:20.350
They were called and we call
them except that water Lords

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:23.243
might be more descriptive or tree Lords.

00:26:24.100 --> 00:26:27.740
And also some lenders who
financed caravan trips to and from

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:28.573
the valley.

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:31.500
This last group is barely known to us

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:35.000
from a couple of contracts
next slide please.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:35.833
And they may have,

00:26:35.833 --> 00:26:39.080
in some cases have been the
same as the owners of wells,

00:26:39.080 --> 00:26:40.090
but in many instances,

00:26:40.090 --> 00:26:43.290
they were probably intermediaries
between the landlords and

00:26:43.290 --> 00:26:47.043
the transporters serving
in effect as brokers.

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:52.390
In this society of the missing
middle is filled mainly

00:26:52.390 --> 00:26:54.083
with artisans and managers,

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:57.330
perhaps the transport
workers also belong here,

00:26:57.330 --> 00:26:58.623
but I do not think so.

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:02.773
Those whose names are given
all have Egyptian names.

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:05.720
This might seem unimportant,

00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:10.230
but it is not when Rodney Austin
and I analyzed the two key

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:12.940
populations of the Trimithis Ostrica cup.

00:27:12.940 --> 00:27:15.560
We found that the tenants whose
names appeared on the wine

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:19.450
jar tags virtually all had Egyptian names.

00:27:19.450 --> 00:27:20.403
Next slide, please.

00:27:22.918 --> 00:27:24.800
The managers in contrast,

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:28.110
almost all had Greek or Roman names.

00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:29.343
The next slide, please.

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:32.730
The contrast is stark.

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:35.760
And I must admit that it surprised us.

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:38.500
We are accustomed to great
caution in the use of names as

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:42.690
evidence after 600 years of
Greek and Roman rule with all of

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:45.950
the cultural complexity that
this brought not to speak of

00:27:45.950 --> 00:27:48.853
intermarriage of the immigrant
populations with locals.

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:51.510
In the valley, we do not find

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:53.563
such stark differences in naming.

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:57.710
Now we know nothing of the
genetic background of these Oases

00:27:57.710 --> 00:28:00.600
groups in the fourth century oases,

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:02.520
but from a cultural standpoint,

00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:05.360
it is clear that they are very distinct.

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:08.440
The managerial class with
its classical education,

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:11.590
for which we have a lot
of evidence in oases

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:15.360
names its children,
accordingly, next slide please.

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:17.350
And this at least ought to
make the class assists in the

00:28:17.350 --> 00:28:18.440
audience, feel at home,

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:21.313
even if Egypt is otherwise alien to them,

00:28:26.600 --> 00:28:27.670
just to be clear,

00:28:27.670 --> 00:28:30.240
I will say that I'm not suggesting
that we should return to

00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:33.160
the view of each Egypt
a protal feudal society,

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:36.280
that pepper ecologists have
been struggling to escape ever

00:28:36.280 --> 00:28:39.093
since H.I Bill put it
forward a century ago.

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:42.580
Most historians today would
accept the view that late

00:28:42.580 --> 00:28:46.360
antique Egypt was a more unequal
society than Hellenistic or

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:48.000
early Roman Egypt.

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:51.250
And that this inequality
rested to a large degree on an

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:53.653
increased concentration of land ownership,

00:28:55.810 --> 00:28:58.248
but the rest of the legal and
social baggage of Bell's proto

00:28:58.248 --> 00:29:02.483
feudalism is no longer alive
in contemporary scholarship.

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:08.250
In the Oasis land ownership
per se is not at stake.

00:29:08.250 --> 00:29:11.070
It is Wells and investment
in long maturing tree crops

00:29:11.070 --> 00:29:12.700
that are critical.

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:16.000
This capital intensive economy
does share with the valley

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:19.240
a larger role for
investment in infrastructure

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:22.860
and a monetized market
for high value crops

00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:25.503
than was true in the Egypt
of an earlier period.

00:29:27.080 --> 00:29:30.140
But because of the water
regime and distance,

00:29:30.140 --> 00:29:33.843
as a far pure instance of a
capital dominated society.

00:29:36.610 --> 00:29:39.950
We have no data to allow us
to measure inequality in this

00:29:39.950 --> 00:29:44.123
society, I cannot offer any
Gini coefficients, alas,

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:47.380
but let us look at one
indicator that to my mind

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:49.973
is significant illegitimacy.

00:29:51.470 --> 00:29:53.880
I've discussed this more fully elsewhere.

00:29:53.880 --> 00:29:56.020
Reassessing the argument
made by Herbert Judy

00:29:56.020 --> 00:29:59.220
a half century ago that
illegitimacy in the Pyri

00:29:59.220 --> 00:30:02.610
of Roman Egypt reflects
marriage restrictions imposed

00:30:02.610 --> 00:30:04.660
by the Roman government rather

00:30:04.660 --> 00:30:06.670
than as he put it the loose morals

00:30:06.670 --> 00:30:08.363
of a low class population.

00:30:09.670 --> 00:30:13.930
Now the negative side of his
case is strong people with only

00:30:13.930 --> 00:30:17.260
a mother's name appear
wherever the evidence allows

00:30:17.260 --> 00:30:20.950
us to say to come from
stable domestic relationships

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:23.550
in which the parents simply
are not legally married.

00:30:24.800 --> 00:30:28.113
The positive side of his
argument, however does not stand.

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:36.470
The use of mother's names for
identification persists long

00:30:36.470 --> 00:30:38.380
after the disappearance of the prohibition

00:30:38.380 --> 00:30:40.200
on soldiers marriages,

00:30:40.200 --> 00:30:44.123
or on inter marriage and long
after the nine constitution.

00:30:45.930 --> 00:30:48.660
It is rather I've argued the
product of a society with

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:52.150
significant economic
inequalities that allow men

00:30:52.150 --> 00:30:54.110
of greater means to have families,

00:30:54.110 --> 00:30:56.500
perhaps second families with

00:30:56.500 --> 00:30:57.333
what we would call concubines.

00:30:57.333 --> 00:31:01.780
As it happens there's a lot
of evidence for illegitimacy

00:31:01.780 --> 00:31:03.840
in the Oasis texts,

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:06.800
both in the third to fourth
century archive for the funeral

00:31:06.800 --> 00:31:10.450
workers of and in the
Austriacus from Kelis.

00:31:10.450 --> 00:31:14.260
And Trimithis the incidence
of illegitimacy appears

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:18.013
to be higher than in
contemporary archives valley.

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:23.400
We are of course familiar with
the phenomenon of a society

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:26.170
in which the economically
disadvantaged are less likely to

00:31:26.170 --> 00:31:29.210
marry than those of
higher economic status.

00:31:29.210 --> 00:31:30.043
We live in it,

00:31:31.664 --> 00:31:34.120
but perhaps even more striking
is the picture that I have

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:35.253
sketched of the Oasis.

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:38.310
And equally familiar to us today

00:31:39.620 --> 00:31:41.530
is the role of elite education

00:31:41.530 --> 00:31:44.210
in reproducing inequality from generation

00:31:44.210 --> 00:31:48.740
to generation that the wealthy
of Roman Egypt supported

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:52.140
classical education for
their male offspring

00:31:52.140 --> 00:31:54.030
is by now well-documented.

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:57.353
And we know quite a lot about
that educational process.

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:00.770
What is more striking is
the link between classical

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:05.160
education and the managerial
ranks not only at the top,

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:07.150
but at the local level,

00:32:07.150 --> 00:32:09.720
the close similarity of the
handwriting of the manager

00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:12.370
who maintained the Kelis account book.

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:15.630
And that if one of the writers
of the codex of I Socrates

00:32:15.630 --> 00:32:19.280
tablets found in the same
room is not an accident

00:32:20.740 --> 00:32:22.800
in the recent publication
of a fourth century,

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:26.650
codex of mathematical
problems in sample contracts.

00:32:26.650 --> 00:32:28.190
The next slide, please,

00:32:28.190 --> 00:32:31.360
Alexander Johnson and I have
argued that it is a witness

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:33.723
to the deliberate formation of managers.

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:37.860
The school of management
attended after a primary and even

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:40.593
secondary education in the classics.

00:32:44.287 --> 00:32:47.490
There is no investment
without risk and the wealthy

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:50.060
residents of the valley who
invested in adding to the stock

00:32:50.060 --> 00:32:53.840
of Wells and the Oasis in the
early years under Roman rule

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:56.370
must have taken account of the risks

00:32:56.370 --> 00:32:58.513
that they could recognize at the time.

00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:01.800
These may not have been
entirely the same as those that

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:04.683
affected investment decisions
in the fourth century,

00:33:06.060 --> 00:33:07.040
it was difficult

00:33:10.580 --> 00:33:13.920
to separate out a recognition
of risks from an attempt

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:17.970
to understand why the
Oasis declined steeply

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:19.193
in late antiquity.

00:33:20.210 --> 00:33:23.053
Across Dakhla there is a
horizon of abandonment,

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:26.770
or at least partial
abandonment in the 360s,

00:33:26.770 --> 00:33:30.043
and to reduce level of
occupation afterward,

00:33:31.010 --> 00:33:33.100
we have less good information for Kharga

00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:36.000
but at least the Southern
part of that Oasis has little

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:39.093
to show after the first
decades of the fifth century,

00:33:40.460 --> 00:33:42.283
much the same as true of Baharia.

00:33:45.030 --> 00:33:47.743
There are four main potential
risks that I can see.

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:51.330
The first is the possibility that Wells

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:54.450
will stop producing water or require

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:55.680
so much deepening that they're

00:33:55.680 --> 00:33:57.563
no longer economically viable.

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:00.180
Today's mechanized drilling

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:01.340
can reach lower aquifers than

00:34:01.340 --> 00:34:03.683
the ancient were capable of tapping.

00:34:04.920 --> 00:34:08.280
We do not know what the
lifespan of an ancient well was.

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:12.593
I do not see how, except with
unlikely extensive excavation,

00:34:14.600 --> 00:34:16.823
excuse me, we will ever find out.

00:34:18.260 --> 00:34:19.803
A second risk is markets.

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:24.780
If demand for Oasis products
were to fall or prices to

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:28.500
soften because of increased
competition from cultivation in

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:30.610
the Highlander, along the valley,

00:34:30.610 --> 00:34:33.490
the profitability of the
enterprise could be threatened

00:34:33.490 --> 00:34:35.310
because high transportation costs

00:34:35.310 --> 00:34:38.323
would he rode profit margins
and harm competitiveness.

00:34:40.180 --> 00:34:42.593
Third risk is the security of the oases.

00:34:43.890 --> 00:34:47.180
We do know in fact that there
were some attacks on Kharga

00:34:47.180 --> 00:34:50.320
in later centuries and
insecurity may have started

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:52.173
to be a factor in the third century.

00:34:53.140 --> 00:34:56.000
It was under the tetrarchy that
the first permanent military

00:34:56.000 --> 00:34:57.793
installations in the Oases appear

00:34:57.793 --> 00:35:01.570
to have been built more
or less simultaneously

00:35:01.570 --> 00:35:04.080
in Baharia, Dakhla and Kharga.

00:35:04.080 --> 00:35:05.203
Next slide, please,

00:35:06.790 --> 00:35:10.470
whether these were responding
to pass the tax or represent a

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:14.330
more forward-looking
apprehension, we do not know.

00:35:14.330 --> 00:35:16.180
There is next to no documentation

00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:18.423
of desert insecurity in this period.

00:35:20.930 --> 00:35:23.360
Finally, it is also
possible that the insecurity

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:25.560
of lines of communication,

00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:27.910
the caravan routes themselves might

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:28.950
have threatened commerce.

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:31.453
Even if the oases themselves were secure,

00:35:32.340 --> 00:35:34.210
there is no chain of military posts

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:36.030
in the desert to secure the roads.

00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:39.650
And we have no way of if
increasing attacks on caravans

00:35:39.650 --> 00:35:42.610
might have raised effective
cost of goods beyond

00:35:42.610 --> 00:35:44.020
what it was possible to recover

00:35:44.020 --> 00:35:46.250
in the markets of the valley.

00:35:46.250 --> 00:35:49.140
In effect, even without
any system of insurance,

00:35:49.140 --> 00:35:52.560
the risk level for caravan
lands may have risen beyond

00:35:52.560 --> 00:35:53.653
the bearable levels.

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:59.250
We lack the data to assess the
contribution that these risks

00:35:59.250 --> 00:36:02.300
may do abandonment and
thus investment failure

00:36:02.300 --> 00:36:04.163
in the fourth and fifth centuries,

00:36:05.240 --> 00:36:08.160
the relative speed and
simultaneity of abandonment at

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:11.480
different sites in Dakhla in the 360s,

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:15.430
makes me doubt that failure
of the Wells was at stake.

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:17.130
It is not likely that the water level

00:36:17.130 --> 00:36:18.740
would have fallen so rapidly.

00:36:18.740 --> 00:36:20.603
And in so many different areas.

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:24.520
The lifespan of Wells may
well have caused investors

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:27.370
to factor that in when looking at return,

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:29.070
but it is not likely to have been a source

00:36:29.070 --> 00:36:30.653
of catastrophic risk.

00:36:32.030 --> 00:36:34.900
Shifts in prices and
competitiveness are also not

00:36:34.900 --> 00:36:38.530
likely to have been so sunken though

00:36:38.530 --> 00:36:40.660
they may indeed have happened.

00:36:40.660 --> 00:36:42.350
For these reasons I believe security

00:36:42.350 --> 00:36:44.490
is a more likely candidate,

00:36:44.490 --> 00:36:46.970
but whether the Oasis
were directly attacked

00:36:46.970 --> 00:36:49.750
and there is no evidence for
this in the fourth century

00:36:49.750 --> 00:36:51.350
and the abandoned sites show,

00:36:51.350 --> 00:36:54.470
no sign of disturbance
or the caravan routes

00:36:54.470 --> 00:36:56.060
were subject to plundering.

00:36:56.060 --> 00:36:57.393
It is hard to say.

00:36:59.410 --> 00:37:02.600
We also cannot at this point
say whether investors could

00:37:02.600 --> 00:37:06.410
have seen these risks coming
already a century earlier,

00:37:06.410 --> 00:37:08.950
or if they became visible only suddenly

00:37:08.950 --> 00:37:10.750
in the middle of the fourth century,

00:37:12.260 --> 00:37:15.130
nor yet do we know how long
the Roman army remained

00:37:15.130 --> 00:37:16.737
in the oases spots.

00:37:17.619 --> 00:37:20.740
They were certainly
still there in the 360s,

00:37:20.740 --> 00:37:23.293
but for how much longer is not clear.

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:27.670
Quses in south Kharga probably
still had some soldiers

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:30.800
in the first two decades
of the early fifth century,

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:33.053
but it was not a major Garrison site.

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:39.740
A lot of things had to come together

00:37:39.740 --> 00:37:41.190
for the economic growth visible

00:37:41.190 --> 00:37:44.700
in the Oasis in the Roman
period to have been possible.

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:47.110
And obviously they did,

00:37:47.110 --> 00:37:50.600
most of them had been in place
already for a half millennium

00:37:50.600 --> 00:37:54.350
and they had undeniably led
to an expansion of settlement,

00:37:54.350 --> 00:37:58.490
water technology, a long
distance transportation,

00:37:58.490 --> 00:38:01.893
a distinctive niche in the
production of valuable crops.

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:06.000
It's possible that the Roman
period brought new crops in the

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:09.360
form of cotton and perhaps
millet, but above all,

00:38:09.360 --> 00:38:11.150
it brought a tax regime favorable

00:38:11.150 --> 00:38:13.223
to the investment of private capital.

00:38:14.540 --> 00:38:16.340
That capital found the combination

00:38:16.340 --> 00:38:18.023
of the other factors attractive.

00:38:19.030 --> 00:38:21.730
It was not until the Egyptian
government in the time of

00:38:21.730 --> 00:38:25.450
Nassar decided to expand
settlement in the Oasis that the

00:38:25.450 --> 00:38:29.373
oases would again be as densely
settled as in Roman times,

00:38:32.000 --> 00:38:35.003
but that visible prosperity
came at a human cost.

00:38:36.430 --> 00:38:39.090
Part of that cost was a
high level of inequality

00:38:39.090 --> 00:38:41.160
and a segmentation of the population.

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:43.823
Very different from what
we find in the Nile valley.

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:46.940
The key to the viability of the system

00:38:46.940 --> 00:38:48.830
was a state management,

00:38:48.830 --> 00:38:52.410
and that was possible only with
the literate tools required

00:38:52.410 --> 00:38:54.763
for recordkeeping and reporting.

00:38:55.660 --> 00:38:58.180
Teachers of the classics
found a ready audience

00:38:58.180 --> 00:39:01.000
in the future agents of capital.

00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:03.520
You can draw any moral for yourself.

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:04.353
Thank you.

00:39:08.120 --> 00:39:10.510
- Possibly the sound of one
hand clapping there Roger,

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:11.803
thank you so much.

00:39:14.350 --> 00:39:19.240
I think Joe is going to
take help manage questions.

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:21.320
How do you wanna do it, Joe?

00:39:21.320 --> 00:39:24.290
- I think if you, if you
all don't mind questions via

00:39:24.290 --> 00:39:26.240
the chat so I can,

00:39:26.240 --> 00:39:28.950
with my colleagues fill them
maybe consolidate a fill them

00:39:28.950 --> 00:39:32.160
to get together in a good single question.

00:39:32.160 --> 00:39:34.150
That sort of thing, just to,

00:39:34.150 --> 00:39:37.380
we don't bombard Roger
from all over the place.

00:39:37.380 --> 00:39:40.010
So if you don't mind, we'll
let Roger rest for 30 seconds,

00:39:40.010 --> 00:39:42.590
catch his breath, then send your questions

00:39:42.590 --> 00:39:44.003
via the chat, please.

00:39:49.319 --> 00:39:50.152
- In the meantime, I should,

00:39:50.152 --> 00:39:52.010
I can say what I should've said
at the beginning that it is

00:39:52.010 --> 00:39:54.903
actually Rostovzteff's
a 150th birthday today.

00:39:56.900 --> 00:39:58.423
So well done.

00:40:00.270 --> 00:40:02.020
- Maybe Roger just myself,

00:40:02.020 --> 00:40:04.750
to get things going for a few
minutes and ask you about the

00:40:04.750 --> 00:40:07.140
current work that you're
doing out there and

00:40:08.329 --> 00:40:09.860
what is on your research agenda

00:40:09.860 --> 00:40:14.130
that you are looking for
that might help flesh out

00:40:14.130 --> 00:40:17.510
this rise in decline story in the oases,

00:40:17.510 --> 00:40:19.563
which is rather dramatic?

00:40:20.700 --> 00:40:25.013
- Oh, well, we'd like to
keep digging of course.

00:40:26.881 --> 00:40:29.810
And as anybody who works in Egypt knows

00:40:29.810 --> 00:40:32.420
that's been impossible in recent years

00:40:32.420 --> 00:40:37.420
to get permission to
excavate in the Oasis.

00:40:37.920 --> 00:40:41.740
They are in the military zone
and the military has not been

00:40:41.740 --> 00:40:43.540
giving permission,

00:40:43.540 --> 00:40:46.370
even though our permission
from the Antiquities Ministry

00:40:46.370 --> 00:40:48.070
has come through every year.

00:40:48.070 --> 00:40:50.520
We have not actually been
able to go out and dig.

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:55.823
At the point that we were stopped.

00:40:56.730 --> 00:41:01.280
We were beginning a house
excavated house that is much

00:41:01.280 --> 00:41:05.430
larger than the rather
large and lavish house

00:41:05.430 --> 00:41:07.110
that we excavated at the beginning,

00:41:07.110 --> 00:41:11.053
the house of Serenas with its
wall painting and so forth.

00:41:12.150 --> 00:41:15.930
And this larger house I think
has got to be at least twice,

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:20.080
if not three times the size of
sereneness with much grander

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:25.080
and better built banquet hall
and a lot of wall paintings

00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:28.420
and much, much higher quality plaster,

00:41:28.420 --> 00:41:30.640
moldings and stuff like this.

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:35.640
So I would like to know whose that is.

00:41:35.980 --> 00:41:40.083
It has an enormous number
of Austriacus well tags,

00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:46.610
these labels from wine jars
that we found in the first year

00:41:46.610 --> 00:41:49.750
we dug in and that was the
last year we were able to work.

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:53.500
So we have a huge amount
still to do in that house.

00:41:53.500 --> 00:41:55.870
And we would really
love to get back to it,

00:41:55.870 --> 00:42:00.870
to get another person from that
upper end of society at the

00:42:01.210 --> 00:42:05.520
same time, there's a lot more
to be done in humbler areas.

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:10.020
And there's an area that seems
to be a century older than

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:14.790
these houses in another part
of the site that we surveyed.

00:42:14.790 --> 00:42:18.610
So some years ago, after
the dunes, March, south,

00:42:18.610 --> 00:42:22.120
and uncovered it for us where
we would like also to work

00:42:22.120 --> 00:42:25.150
because that area looks to have
much more like what we would

00:42:25.150 --> 00:42:27.450
think of as Roman urban planning,

00:42:27.450 --> 00:42:31.910
but the houses are also more
modest and we'd like to see

00:42:31.910 --> 00:42:35.650
what kind of people live there to get a

00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:37.870
a better sense of

00:42:37.870 --> 00:42:40.200
is there really a middle
class that we're just totally

00:42:40.200 --> 00:42:42.340
missing up to this point

00:42:42.340 --> 00:42:44.140
from the sites that we've worked at?

00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:48.270
The Egyptians have also been
working at another site.

00:42:48.270 --> 00:42:51.280
I know Seville, which has yielded Ostrica.

00:42:51.280 --> 00:42:55.330
That seemed pretty much to
reflect the kind of dare I say it

00:42:55.330 --> 00:42:59.750
today, plantation economy
that I've been describing.

00:42:59.750 --> 00:43:04.580
And if we were able to work in Dakhla

00:43:04.580 --> 00:43:08.020
it would be a lot easier to
collaborate with them because

00:43:08.020 --> 00:43:10.560
they've been very open about
allowing us to work on the

00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:12.123
documents they've found.

00:43:16.510 --> 00:43:19.190
- Okay, the questions are
kind of rolling in now.

00:43:19.190 --> 00:43:22.553
So if I may, let me get to some of them.

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:26.950
Bear with me, could a
shift from bank credit

00:43:26.950 --> 00:43:29.710
in the third century CE
to peer-to-peer credit

00:43:29.710 --> 00:43:31.370
in the fifth and sixth century CE

00:43:31.370 --> 00:43:33.273
have been a factor in decline.

00:43:36.800 --> 00:43:38.990
- I know that peer-to-peer credit

00:43:38.990 --> 00:43:40.700
would have been a factor in decline.

00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:44.710
We certainly do find it
in earlier centuries.

00:43:44.710 --> 00:43:48.590
That's just that we find
more of it and we don't.

00:43:48.590 --> 00:43:52.180
And I don't think we find
significant bank credit

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:54.680
in that fashion at all.

00:43:54.680 --> 00:43:59.680
Actually, I don't think that
credit was in short supply,

00:44:00.880 --> 00:44:05.240
but it's possible that the
water Lords were more rapacious

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:09.590
with it and it's really very hard to say

00:44:09.590 --> 00:44:11.980
because we don't have
the comparable documents

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:14.270
from the earlier period.

00:44:14.270 --> 00:44:17.500
So we don't see credit
transactions happening in the Oasis

00:44:17.500 --> 00:44:18.733
in an earlier time.

00:44:22.625 --> 00:44:24.964
- If the basis of the inequality
was structurally different,

00:44:24.964 --> 00:44:27.550
how did this express itself, was it last,

00:44:27.550 --> 00:44:29.211
more simply shifted?

00:44:29.211 --> 00:44:32.961
Do you think it felt
qualitatively different?

00:44:34.617 --> 00:44:37.686
- I'm sorry, I didn't didn't
hear that clearly Joe.

00:44:37.686 --> 00:44:40.422
- If the basis of the inequality
was structurally different,

00:44:40.422 --> 00:44:41.517
how did this express itself,

00:44:41.517 --> 00:44:43.900
was it less more simply shifted?

00:44:43.900 --> 00:44:46.393
Do you think it felt
qualitatively different?

00:44:48.100 --> 00:44:50.260
- Well, I think the missing middle

00:44:50.260 --> 00:44:51.960
made it qualitatively different.

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:54.760
We didn't have as much of a continuum

00:44:54.760 --> 00:44:56.330
from the poor to the rich

00:44:57.810 --> 00:45:02.810
the Gulf was much wider and
the managers were, if anything,

00:45:02.820 --> 00:45:04.780
on the rich side of that divide, right?

00:45:04.780 --> 00:45:09.030
Because they got educated and
no doubt took off their share

00:45:09.030 --> 00:45:10.700
of the revenues.

00:45:10.700 --> 00:45:13.820
Whereas I think the transporters
were probably on the wrong

00:45:13.820 --> 00:45:17.110
side of that line, but
without the middle group,

00:45:17.110 --> 00:45:20.620
I think that the extremity of it.

00:45:20.620 --> 00:45:23.253
The quantity is the quality in that sense.

00:45:27.150 --> 00:45:28.820
- Do we have any contracts governing

00:45:28.820 --> 00:45:31.650
the tenancy arrangements
where these bound tenants

00:45:31.650 --> 00:45:35.110
at scripted, where they paid wages,

00:45:35.110 --> 00:45:38.440
where they debt slaves also
tree crop production is often

00:45:38.440 --> 00:45:41.670
highly labor intensive water
lifting costs and so on.

00:45:41.670 --> 00:45:43.463
Do we have evidence of slave labor?

00:45:45.110 --> 00:45:48.740
- There is certainly evidence
for slavery in the oases,

00:45:48.740 --> 00:45:53.740
but not as far as I know for
slave labor in agriculture,

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:58.420
we have no tenancy contracts.

00:45:58.420 --> 00:46:02.070
And if I remember correctly,
no real labor contracts,

00:46:02.070 --> 00:46:07.070
either what we have is on the one hand,

00:46:07.480 --> 00:46:09.910
the labels coming in on the line jars,

00:46:09.910 --> 00:46:11.720
showing who the tenants were.

00:46:11.720 --> 00:46:15.260
And on the other hand, the
rent rolls in the account book,

00:46:15.260 --> 00:46:18.720
which were set up with
the names of the tenants,

00:46:18.720 --> 00:46:21.240
which basically are the same
from year to year over a

00:46:21.240 --> 00:46:24.970
three-year period and
laid down in advance,

00:46:24.970 --> 00:46:27.620
the amount of rent that
was expected from them,

00:46:27.620 --> 00:46:31.320
and then entries made
as they made payments.

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:35.230
So it looks to have been an
extremely stable situation with

00:46:35.230 --> 00:46:38.650
not a lot of variety from year to year

00:46:38.650 --> 00:46:40.923
and probably not much flux at all.

00:46:42.100 --> 00:46:44.880
This suggests to me a
situation in which the tenants

00:46:44.880 --> 00:46:46.703
had very limited agency.

00:46:50.070 --> 00:46:53.260
- Two questions after my
own heart at the moment.

00:46:53.260 --> 00:46:55.520
What about climate change

00:46:55.520 --> 00:46:58.473
or disease as factors in decline, Roger.

00:46:59.620 --> 00:47:01.270
- Sorry, climate change, or what?

00:47:02.980 --> 00:47:03.870
what was the second one?

00:47:03.870 --> 00:47:05.290
- [Joe] Or Disease

00:47:05.290 --> 00:47:08.303
- Disease, yes,

00:47:12.100 --> 00:47:16.040
it's very likely that the
Oases were affected as the rest

00:47:16.040 --> 00:47:20.770
of Egypt was by the end of
the Roman climatic optimum,

00:47:20.770 --> 00:47:24.160
but because the water
came from underground

00:47:24.160 --> 00:47:27.650
and had been laid down
thousands of years earlier,

00:47:27.650 --> 00:47:31.390
it is not to have been affected
in the way that the valley

00:47:31.390 --> 00:47:35.300
was by changes in the Nile flood

00:47:35.300 --> 00:47:38.980
as a result of rainfall patterns shifting.

00:47:38.980 --> 00:47:43.060
So I doubt that that
climate change is likely

00:47:43.060 --> 00:47:45.140
to have made a lot of difference.

00:47:45.140 --> 00:47:49.180
I don't think changes in
temperature would have been

00:47:49.180 --> 00:47:52.240
particularly harmful because
the Oases are in general,

00:47:52.240 --> 00:47:54.220
hotter than the valley anyway.

00:47:54.220 --> 00:47:56.990
So if they'd cooled off just a few degrees

00:47:56.990 --> 00:47:57.823
in the fourth century,

00:47:57.823 --> 00:47:59.230
that probably would've been a good thing

00:47:59.230 --> 00:48:00.663
from their point of view.

00:48:01.760 --> 00:48:05.730
Disease is harder to say there certainly

00:48:05.730 --> 00:48:09.140
was plenty of disease
and also plenty of trauma

00:48:11.220 --> 00:48:15.100
that we can find from the
work of the bio archeologists

00:48:15.100 --> 00:48:19.090
at Kelis in particular, but also at Aldair

00:48:19.090 --> 00:48:21.913
and at Labaca in Kharga.

00:48:23.710 --> 00:48:28.710
But the chronology of the
burials is not precise

00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:34.260
enough as far as I can see to
give us a sense of change over

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:37.320
time in what diseases were there

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:39.060
and the diseases that they have found

00:48:39.060 --> 00:48:42.740
mostly don't look as if they are likely

00:48:42.740 --> 00:48:46.387
to have been specific
epidemics, let us say,

00:48:48.130 --> 00:48:51.610
I don't think we've got enough evidence

00:48:51.610 --> 00:48:56.610
for the second half of the second century

00:48:57.200 --> 00:48:59.930
to hope to nail smallpox there.

00:48:59.930 --> 00:49:01.050
And on the other hand,

00:49:01.050 --> 00:49:04.490
we don't have cemeteries excavated so far

00:49:04.490 --> 00:49:06.010
that date to the sixth century,

00:49:06.010 --> 00:49:08.250
where we might find bubonic plague.

00:49:08.250 --> 00:49:10.410
So most of the evidence that we have

00:49:10.410 --> 00:49:12.290
is in a chronological fork

00:49:12.290 --> 00:49:14.683
that's not favorable to
finding that evidence.

00:49:18.770 --> 00:49:20.283
- Who owned the camels?

00:49:21.978 --> 00:49:23.800
Hello, Jim.

00:49:23.800 --> 00:49:28.800
- That is very hard to say actually it.

00:49:29.366 --> 00:49:32.510
- And operators hire drivers
in the trucking industry.

00:49:32.510 --> 00:49:34.140
So can we differentiate?

00:49:34.140 --> 00:49:39.010
- Yeah it is perfectly
possible that those existed

00:49:40.020 --> 00:49:43.060
that some of the camels belonged

00:49:43.060 --> 00:49:47.160
to the capitalists and that they

00:49:48.610 --> 00:49:51.390
then that they hired Camel drivers

00:49:51.390 --> 00:49:54.940
to send their stuff to their markets.

00:49:54.940 --> 00:49:55.773
On the other hand,

00:49:55.773 --> 00:50:00.260
they contracts in Genoa that
I republished in the Gusscu

00:50:00.260 --> 00:50:04.920
festschrift it looks as if the
transporters are themselves,

00:50:04.920 --> 00:50:09.920
the camel owners, and they
are kind of independent agents

00:50:10.100 --> 00:50:15.100
contracting with a
capitalist for the journey.

00:50:15.280 --> 00:50:16.860
There's a lot, that's unclear in those.

00:50:16.860 --> 00:50:19.290
They're not very well drafted,

00:50:19.290 --> 00:50:22.420
but it looks as if that is more nearly

00:50:22.420 --> 00:50:24.510
an arm's length relationship,

00:50:24.510 --> 00:50:27.530
even though the same
people clearly contracted

00:50:27.530 --> 00:50:28.773
in more than one year.

00:50:31.735 --> 00:50:33.730
- If you would take
one more question maybe

00:50:33.730 --> 00:50:35.840
at the late hour here,

00:50:35.840 --> 00:50:40.330
what were the unique characteristics
of the share contracts

00:50:40.330 --> 00:50:43.410
between the water Lords and
water infrastructure ownership

00:50:43.410 --> 00:50:46.960
compared to other sorts of proto
corporative contracts found

00:50:46.960 --> 00:50:49.810
in other parts of Egypt or
the rest of the Mediterranean?

00:50:50.700 --> 00:50:54.490
- Sorry, I lost the second half of that.

00:50:54.490 --> 00:50:56.240
- Were there any unique characteristics

00:50:56.240 --> 00:50:58.720
of the shared contracts
between the water Lords

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:03.300
and the water infrastructure
ownership compared

00:51:03.300 --> 00:51:06.750
to other sources proto
corporative contracts

00:51:06.750 --> 00:51:08.030
found in other parts of Egypt

00:51:08.030 --> 00:51:10.120
or the rest of the Mediterranean?

00:51:10.120 --> 00:51:12.270
- Unfortunately, we
don't have the contracts

00:51:12.270 --> 00:51:15.300
between the water Lords and the tenants.

00:51:15.300 --> 00:51:20.090
All we have is the evidence
in the rent rolls of what was

00:51:20.090 --> 00:51:22.133
going on from year to year.

00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:27.360
So we are left pretty much to guess from

00:51:28.340 --> 00:51:30.910
this external evidence
at what the nature of

00:51:30.910 --> 00:51:35.010
the relationship might be,
this is deeply regrettable.

00:51:35.010 --> 00:51:37.390
I would love to have
that kind of evidence,

00:51:37.390 --> 00:51:41.380
but it really looks as if it
is something close to customary

00:51:41.380 --> 00:51:43.530
and even hereditary tendency.

00:51:43.530 --> 00:51:46.900
There's one case where
it looks as if it passes

00:51:46.900 --> 00:51:49.940
from one member of a family to another,

00:51:49.940 --> 00:51:52.710
between two years and the account book.

00:51:52.710 --> 00:51:56.460
So I suspect it's very much in

00:51:56.460 --> 00:51:58.333
that kind of hereditary frame.

00:52:03.060 --> 00:52:04.720
- Okay well, Roger, thank you so much.

00:52:04.720 --> 00:52:08.110
I think I'd turn it over
to our chair, Chris,

00:52:08.110 --> 00:52:11.840
who would invite us for
a wonderful cocktail

00:52:11.840 --> 00:52:15.130
in front of a beautiful
fireplace (Joe laughs)

00:52:15.130 --> 00:52:16.343
in new Haven, but.

00:52:19.420 --> 00:52:21.320
- I'm gonna go have a glass of Bandol.

00:52:23.020 --> 00:52:24.130
- That sounds very nice.

00:52:24.130 --> 00:52:28.850
I actually asked Noel if he
would just do the end bit.

00:52:28.850 --> 00:52:29.883
- Oh yeah, okay.

00:52:32.296 --> 00:52:36.040
- I'm happy to chime in here
after such a wonderful lecture.

00:52:36.040 --> 00:52:38.630
And right before the lecture began,

00:52:38.630 --> 00:52:41.520
I had sent an email to
Roger and some of the other

00:52:41.520 --> 00:52:44.210
co-organizers talking
about how on some level,

00:52:44.210 --> 00:52:47.710
I think we probably all feel
like we've been living in Oasis

00:52:47.710 --> 00:52:49.330
since last March.

00:52:49.330 --> 00:52:54.000
All of us isolated in our little enclaves,

00:52:54.000 --> 00:52:56.560
hopefully with family and friends,

00:52:56.560 --> 00:53:00.640
but in so far as that's true,
I can say that this evening,

00:53:00.640 --> 00:53:05.510
at least we've witnessed
Roger kind of bring us drinks

00:53:05.510 --> 00:53:08.950
of cool intellectual water
and moreover to do things

00:53:08.950 --> 00:53:10.690
in a manner that didn't obtain

00:53:10.690 --> 00:53:14.670
in the Western oases in so
far as he's been so generous.

00:53:14.670 --> 00:53:16.520
He's not a capitalist with his knowledge.

00:53:16.520 --> 00:53:18.050
He's great at sharing it out.

00:53:18.050 --> 00:53:20.470
So I think we can thank
him very much for all that

00:53:20.470 --> 00:53:22.130
he's taught us tonight and all

00:53:22.130 --> 00:53:24.303
that he's done to enlighten us.

00:53:25.520 --> 00:53:26.353
- [Roger] Thank.

00:53:27.417 --> 00:53:30.350
- And we should know that we
hope to have the host Rodger in

00:53:30.350 --> 00:53:33.710
the fall, in the Beinecke
to have a kind of a,

00:53:33.710 --> 00:53:37.100
a pro seminar with Papyri
actually in person.

00:53:37.100 --> 00:53:39.870
We are keeping fingers crossed on that.

00:53:39.870 --> 00:53:41.160
- Inshallah.

00:53:41.160 --> 00:53:42.360
- [Joe] Inshallah. Yeah.

00:53:44.700 --> 00:53:46.130
- Goodnight, everyone.

00:53:46.130 --> 00:53:46.963
- [Roger] All right.

00:53:47.930 --> 00:53:48.860
- Thank you so much.

00:53:48.860 --> 00:53:52.393
It's nice to see old
friends and new friends.

